	abstract = {Battery-powered IoT nodes are increasingly vulnerable to physical attacks such as fault injection, probing, and side-channel analysis. Traditional countermeasures either incur high area/power overhead or address only a single threat class. This paper introduces LightShield, a unified VLSI architecture integrating (1) a dynamic-threshold Anomaly Detection Unit (ADU), (2) a dual-rail, randomized Active Shield Controller (ASC), and (3) a Hamming (7,4)-based Error Detection \& Response (EDR). We prototype LightShield on a Xilinx Artix-7 FPGA for real-time fault injection and side-channel measurement, then perform post-layout simulations in a 65 nm CMOS process. Experimental results show 94.5\% fault coverage with only 2.3 \% false positives, an 18.3 \% dynamic power overhead (56.2 μW total), a mean response latency of 38.7 ns, 0.19 CPA correlation, and 12.6\% area overhead significantly improving security over a Hamming-only baseline. LightShield delivers robust, low-power protection suitable for next-generation secure IoT deployments.},
